<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdirashid Ali Shermarke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdirashid Ali Shermarke was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> politician, prime minister from 1960 to 1964 and president of the Somali Republic from 1967. His governments pursued the union of all Somali-inhabited lands, backing insurgents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-frontier-district">Northern Frontier District</a>. He was assassinated by one of his bodyguards on 15 October 1969, and six days later the army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> seized power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdirashid-ali-shermarke.jpg" alt="Abdirashid Ali Shermarke" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Somalia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdirashid_Ali_Shermarke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke was born on 8 June 1919 at Harardhere in the Mudug region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. His father belonged to the Osman Mohamud Majeerteen branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a> clan and his mother to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habar-gidir">Habar Gidir</a> clan. He was raised in Mogadishu by his mother, attended Qur'anic schools and finished elementary education in 1936.</p><p>He then worked as a trader and as a civil servant in the Italian colonial administration. In 1943 he joined the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-youth-league">Somali Youth League</a>, and the next year he entered the British administration's civil service. He completed secondary school in 1953 and went on a scholarship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapienza-university-of-rome">Sapienza University of Rome</a>, where he took a degree in political science.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1959, Sharmarke was elected to the Legislative Assembly. British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> became independent on 26 June 1960 and joined the Trust Territory of Somaliland, formerly Italian Somaliland, on 1 July 1960 to form the Somali Republic. On that day President Aden Abdullah Osman Daar appointed him the first Prime Minister. He travelled widely in pursuit of a non-aligned and neutral foreign policy, and remained in office until March 1964, when the first general elections returned him to Parliament as a member. In the presidential election of 1967 he defeated Daar and was sworn in as the second President on 6 July 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1968 Sharmarke narrowly survived an assassination attempt when a grenade exploded near the car carrying him from the airport. On 15 October 1969, during an official visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-anod">Las Anod</a>, he was shot dead at the guest house by one of his own bodyguards, who fired an automatic rifle at close range. Observers suggested personal rather than political motives. On 21 October 1969, the day after his funeral, the Somali Army under Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> seized power in an almost bloodless coup. His son Omar Abdirashid Ali Sharmarke, born in 1960, later became Prime Minister of the Somali Transitional Federal Government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdirashid Shermarke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 21 October 1969, days after the assassination of President <b>Abdirashid Ali Shermarke</b>, the army led by Mohamed Siad Barre seized power in the Somali Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/somali-democratic-republic">Somali Democratic Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abdirashid Ali Shermarke</b> won the presidency in 1967.</blockquote><a href="/state/somali-republic">Somali Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Harardhere, in what is now <a href="/countries/somalia">Somalia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/las-anod">Las Anod</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.65,47.86&amp;year=1951" aria-label="Harardhere on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5877 1990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abdirashid Ali Shermarke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6327 2260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6327 2260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.65,47.86&amp;year=1951">What was here in 1951? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
